concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-2-sec-9 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "9 Meaning of safety defect in relation to goods"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-2-sec-9 ;
    values:originalText "(1) For the purposes of this Schedule, goods have a safety defect if their safety is not such as persons generally are entitled to expect.  (2) In determining the extent of the safety of goods, regard is to be given to all relevant circumstances, including:  (a) the manner in which, and the purposes for which, they have been marketed; and  (b) their packaging; and  (c) the use of any mark in relation to them; and  (d) any instructions for, or warnings with respect to, doing, or refraining from doing, anything with or in relation to them; and  (e) what might reasonably be expected to be done with or in relation to them; and  (f) the time when they were supplied by their manufacturer.  (3) An inference that goods have a safety defect is not to be made only because of the fact that, after they were supplied by their manufacturer, safer goods of the same kind were supplied.  (4) An inference that goods have a safety defect is not to be made only because:  (a) there was compliance with a Commonwealth mandatory standard for them; and  (b) that standard was not the safest possible standard having regard to the latest state of scientific or technical knowledge when they were supplied by their manufacturer.  The Australian Consumer Law  Schedule 2 Introduction  Chapter 1  Section 10  Competition and Consumer Act 2010  Compilation date: 27/05/2026" ;
    skos:definition "(1) For the purposes of this Schedule, goods have a safety defect if their safety is not such as persons generally are entitled to expect.  (2) In determining the extent of the safety of goods, regard is to be given to all relevant circumstances, including:  (a) the manner in which, and the purposes for which, they have been marketed; and  (b) their packaging; and  (c) the use of any mark in relation to them; and  (d) any instructions for, or warnings with respect to, doing, or refraining from doing, anything with or in relation to them; and  (e) what might reasonably be expected to be done with or in relation to them; and  (f) the time when they were supplied by their manufacturer.  (3) An inference that goods have a safety defect is not to be made only because of the fact that, after they were supplied by their manufacturer, safer goods of the same kind were supplied.  (4) An inference that goods have a safety defect is not to be made only because:  (a) there was compliance with a Commonwealth mandatory standard for them; and  (b) that standard was not the safest possible standard having regard to the latest state of scientific or technical knowledge when they were supplied by their manufacturer.  The Australian Consumer Law  Schedule 2 Introduction  Chapter 1  Section 10  Competition and Consumer Act 2010  Compilation date: 27/05/2026"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "7973935931c5dcc93d70c0bc272041943b5ca45469095034f39717599c83149f" ;
    cof:pageNumber "114"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-2-sec-9-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-2-sec-9-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-2-sec-9-ss-3 ;
