concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-2-sec-21 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "21 Unconscionable conduct in connection with goods or services"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-2-sec-21 ;
    values:originalText "(1) A person must not, in trade or commerce, in connection with:  (a) the supply or possible supply of goods or services to a person; or  (b) the acquisition or possible acquisition of goods or services from a person; engage in conduct that is, in all the circumstances, unconscionable.  (2) This section does not apply to conduct that is engaged in only because the person engaging in the conduct:  (a) institutes legal proceedings in relation to the supply or possible supply, or in relation to the acquisition or possible acquisition; or  (b) refers to arbitration a dispute or claim in relation to the supply or possible supply, or in relation to the acquisition or possible acquisition.  (3) For the purpose of determining whether a person has contravened subsection (1):  (a) the court must not have regard to any circumstances that were not reasonably foreseeable at the time of the alleged contravention; and  Schedule 2  The Australian Consumer Law Chapter 2  General protections" ;
    skos:definition "(1) A person must not, in trade or commerce, in connection with:  (a) the supply or possible supply of goods or services to a person; or  (b) the acquisition or possible acquisition of goods or services from a person; engage in conduct that is, in all the circumstances, unconscionable.  (2) This section does not apply to conduct that is engaged in only because the person engaging in the conduct:  (a) institutes legal proceedings in relation to the supply or possible supply, or in relation to the acquisition or possible acquisition; or  (b) refers to arbitration a dispute or claim in relation to the supply or possible supply, or in relation to the acquisition or possible acquisition.  (3) For the purpose of determining whether a person has contravened subsection (1):  (a) the court must not have regard to any circumstances that were not reasonably foreseeable at the time of the alleged contravention; and  Schedule 2  The Australian Consumer Law Chapter 2  General protections"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "253c15994ddfe660329798b65390a96b66fbe637dd6d590336a7cfd6378483c1" ;
    cof:pageNumber "123"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-2-sec-21-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-2-sec-21-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-2-sec-21-ss-3 ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf> ;
    dc:source "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.123" .
doc:sch-2-sec-21 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "21 Unconscionable conduct in connection with goods or services" ;
    values:kind "section" ;
    values:originalText "(1) A person must not, in trade or commerce, in connection with:  (a) the supply or possible supply of goods or services to a person; or  (b) the acquisition or possible acquisition of goods or services from a person; engage in conduct that is, in all the circumstances, unconscionable.  (2) This section does not apply to conduct that is engaged in only because the person engaging in the conduct:  (a) institutes legal proceedings in relation to the supply or possible supply, or in relation to the acquisition or possible acquisition; or  (b) refers to arbitration a dispute or claim in relation to the supply or possible supply, or in relation to the acquisition or possible acquisition.  (3) For the purpose of determining whether a person has contravened subsection (1):  (a) the court must not have regard to any circumstances that were not reasonably foreseeable at the time of the alleged contravention; and  Schedule 2  The Australian Consumer Law Chapter 2  General protections" ;
    cof:pageNumber "123"^^xsd:integer .

