concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sch-2-sec-213 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "213 Preference must be given to compensation for victims"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-2-sec-213 ;
    values:originalText "If a court considers that:  (a) it is appropriate to impose a fine on a person (the defendant) under this Chapter in relation to:  (i) a contravention of a provision of this Schedule; or  (ii) an attempt to contravene such a provision; or  (iii) aiding, abetting, counselling or procuring a person to contravene such a provision; or  (iv) inducing, or attempting to induce, a person, whether by threats or promises or otherwise, to contravene such a provision; or  (v) being in any way, directly or indirectly, knowingly concerned in, or party to, the contravention by a person of such a provision; or  (vi) conspiring with others to contravene such a provision; and  (b) it is appropriate to order the defendant to pay compensation to a person who has suffered loss or damage as result of that contravention or conduct; and  (c) the defendant does not have sufficient financial resources to pay both the fine and the compensation; the court must give preference to making an order for compensation.  Schedule 2  The Australian Consumer Law Chapter 4  Offences" ;
    skos:definition "If a court considers that:  (a) it is appropriate to impose a fine on a person (the defendant) under this Chapter in relation to:  (i) a contravention of a provision of this Schedule; or  (ii) an attempt to contravene such a provision; or  (iii) aiding, abetting, counselling or procuring a person to contravene such a provision; or  (iv) inducing, or attempting to induce, a person, whether by threats or promises or otherwise, to contravene such a provision; or  (v) being in any way, directly or indirectly, knowingly concerned in, or party to, the contravention by a person of such a provision; or  (vi) conspiring with others to contravene such a provision; and  (b) it is appropriate to order the defendant to pay compensation to a person who has suffered loss or damage as result of that contravention or conduct; and  (c) the defendant does not have sufficient financial resources to pay both the fine and the compensation; the court must give preference to making an order for compensation.  Schedule 2  The Australian Consumer Law Chapter 4  Offences"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "22e5863b4d2a3e47d75e97e09762f4ba21a880b5450cdd0eddf574ad79b2755c" ;
    cof:pageNumber "313"^^xsd:integer ;
    skos:note "Court must give preference to making an order for compensation if defendant cannot pay fine and compensation." ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf> ;
    dc:source "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.313" ;
